        <div id="noaa-alerts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How NOAA Weather Alerts Actually Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            NOAA Weather Radio is a network of transmitters run by the National Weather Service that broadcasts forecasts, watches and warnings around the clock on seven frequencies between 162.400 and 162.550 MHz. Every radio in this guide can receive those seven channels. In most of the US you will hear at least one; in some rural areas and deep valleys, reception can be weak, so it is worth testing your radio at home before you need it.
          </p>

          <p>
            When the Weather Service issues a warning, the transmitter sends an alert tone ahead of the broadcast. A radio with an alert function listens for that tone while otherwise silent and sounds its own siren when it hears it. That is what lets an alert radio wake you for a tornado or flash flood warning at night. Midland describes the ER210’s siren as running for eight seconds, and notes that alerts include the required weekly test.
          </p>

          <p>
            The limitation of crank radios is that their alert is not targeted. Dedicated tabletop weather radios with SAME coding let you program your county so the radio only sounds for warnings that affect you. Crank radios like the ER310 do not offer that; Midland’s FAQ says the ER310 receives all alerts in the area, including the weekly test, and alerts cannot be customized. For a radio that lives in a kit or comes out during storms, that is a fair trade. For a bedside radio that stays on all year, a SAME-coded tabletop model is less likely to wake you for a warning two counties away.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No alert, no warning.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A radio that only receives NOAA broadcasts tells you the forecast when you turn it on. A radio with an alert function wakes you when a warning is issued. If this is your only warning device, buy one with alert.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Phone alerts are good, but they are not a substitute. Wireless Emergency Alerts reach phones through the cellular network, and during major disasters towers can lose power or become overloaded, and phones run flat. A battery-powered alert radio is independent of all of that, which is why emergency managers keep recommending one for every household.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-sources" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Crank, Solar, USB and Batteries: What Each Is Good For
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every radio here advertises multiple power sources, and it is tempting to read them as equal. They are not. In practice the internal battery and the disposable-battery backup do most of the work, USB charging fills the internal battery before and between outages, and the crank and solar panel are there to keep the radio and light going when everything else has run out.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Internal battery</h3>

          <p>
            The rechargeable pack is what you will use most. Charge it from a wall outlet or a power bank before storm season, and it should run the radio for many hours. Midland rates the ER310 for up to 45 hours of normal use and the ER210 for up to 25 hours. Lithium packs slowly lose charge in storage, so top them up every few months.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">AA and AAA backup</h3>

          <p>
            This is the most underrated feature. A radio that takes store-bought cells, like the ER310 with six AAs, the KA500 with three AAs, or the FRX3+ and FosPower A1 with AAAs, can keep going through a long outage on a pack of batteries from the kit. Alkaline cells also keep well in storage, which a lithium pack does not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hand crank</h3>

          <p>
            A crank turns your arm into a small generator. It is reliable and needs no sun, but an arm produces very little electrical power for long, so cranking is best for bringing a flat radio back to life for a broadcast or for a few minutes of light. Our <a href="/best-emergency-lantern/">emergency lantern guide</a> makes the same point about crank lanterns: a minute of cranking typically buys a few minutes of moderate use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solar panel</h3>

          <p>
            The panel on a crank radio is a few square inches. Like the small cells on solar power banks, it trickles rather than charges. Midland’s FAQs say it plainly: on the ER210, solar extends the charge but does not fully charge the radio, and on the ER310 solar alone will not charge a dead battery. Midland adds that a fully charged ER310 left in the sun can last about two to three weeks between charges, versus about a week without sun. That is exactly the right way to use it: as a way to stretch a charged battery, not to fill an empty one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="phone-charging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Truth About Charging a Phone From a Crank Radio
          </h2>

          <p>
            “Emergency radio with phone charger” is one of the most searched phrases in this category, and it is the feature most likely to disappoint. The reason is simple arithmetic. A modern smartphone battery holds more energy than the 2,000 to 2,600mAh packs in most crank radios, and some of that pack is lost in conversion before it reaches the phone. So even a full radio battery delivers a partial phone charge, and draining it to charge a phone leaves the radio with nothing for the next broadcast.
          </p>

          <p>
            The makers are candid about it when you read past the box. Midland’s ER310 FAQ says the radio will not reliably charge newer iPhones or Android phones, that it was designed for emergency-only charging, that it will not charge a device at the expense of its own battery, and that a 2,600mAh battery may barely raise a newer phone’s charge. The ER210’s FAQ similarly warns that many newer iPhones and Android phones may not charge from it. We are not going to invent a minutes-of-cranking-per-percent figure; none of the makers here publishes one for phone charging, and the honest answer is that it is slow enough to be a last resort.
          </p>

          <p>
            Charging a phone from the crank directly is worse still. A phone needs a steady supply to start charging, and an arm cannot crank steadily for long. Owner reviews across this category commonly report that the crank is good for running the radio and light but not for meaningfully charging other electronics.
          </p>

          <p>
            The right setup is to let the radio be a radio and to keep phones alive with a power bank. A 10,000 to 20,000mAh bank holds several phone charges, and our <a href="/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/">guide to portable chargers for power outages</a> sizes banks for how long you need to last. If you want to recharge banks without the grid, our <a href="/best-solar-power-bank/">best solar power bank guide</a> explains why a folding 21W to 28W panel feeding a plain bank beats any built-in solar cell. For a phone that stays in a pocket, a <a href="/best-magsafe-power-bank/">MagSafe power bank</a> is the most convenient top-up.
          </p>

