        <div id="buying-guide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Buying Guide: The Specs That Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            With the phone charger put in its place, the decision comes down to five features. In order of importance: the weather alert, a disposable-battery backup, reception, the light and the size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weather alert</h3>

          <p>
            If the radio may be your only warning device, it must have an automatic NOAA alert. Four of our six picks do. Check that the listing says alert, not just NOAA or weather band, because many budget radios receive the broadcasts without listening for the warning tone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Backup batteries</h3>

          <p>
            AA or AAA support turns a radio with a finite internal pack into one that can run for weeks. AA cells hold more energy than AAAs, which is one reason the ER310 and KA500 are the strongest long-outage radios here.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Reception</h3>

          <p>
            Small radios have small antennas. The larger Kaito and Midland ER310 generally have more room for a better antenna and speaker than pocket radios. Whichever you buy, test it on all seven NOAA channels at home, find the strongest one, and note it on a piece of tape on the radio.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Light</h3>

          <p>
            A radio flashlight is a backup light, not your main one, but quality varies. The ER210’s Cree LED is rated at 130 lumens, enough to move around a dark house. The KA500 and MD-090P add reading lamps, which light a table rather than a spot. For lighting a room through a long outage, a dedicated <a href="/best-emergency-lantern/">emergency lantern</a> is far better.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Size and weight</h3>

          <p>
            For a go-bag or car kit, smaller wins: the ER210, FRX3+ and FosPower A1 all fit in a glovebox. For a household kit that stays at home, the size of the ER310 or KA500 buys a bigger speaker, more power options and, on the Kaito, shortwave.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="kit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Building a Crank Radio Into an Outage Kit
          </h2>

          <p>
            A radio is one piece of a kit, and it works best when the other pieces cover its weaknesses. Light, phone power and information are the three things people run short of first in an outage, and each needs its own tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            For light, pair the radio with a rechargeable lantern and a headlamp; the radio flashlight then becomes a spare. For phone power, keep a charged 20,000mAh power bank in the kit and a smaller magnetic bank in daily use. For information, the radio with alert is the core, and a written list of local NOAA and AM news frequencies taped inside the kit lid saves fumbling in the dark.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you expect outages longer than a couple of days, add a way to recharge from the sun. A folding USB panel, sized as described in our <a href="/best-solar-power-bank/">solar power bank guide</a>, keeps a power bank filling during the day, and that bank can then recharge the radio, the lantern and phones. For longer outages that also involve medical devices, fridges or fans, a <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">portable solar panel</a> paired with a power station is the next step up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance is what makes a kit work. Every few months, charge the radio’s internal battery, check the backup cells, turn on the weather alert and confirm you can hear your local channel. Replace alkaline backup cells on a schedule, and store them outside the radio so a leaking cell cannot damage the contacts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which One to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a household storm kit, buy the Midland ER310. It combines an automatic NOAA alert, a long rated runtime and a six-AA backup that keeps it going when the crank and sun are not enough. Its phone charger is weak, as Midland itself says, but that is not what it is for.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a go-bag, the Eton FRX3+ is the better fit, with the alert and a AAA backup in a smaller, lighter body. If you prefer Midland or cannot find the Eton in stock, the ER210 gives you the same alert in an even smaller package, at the cost of disposable-battery backup.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want shortwave and a reading lamp and do not mind the weight, buy the Kaito KA500, checking the exact variant for its battery and phone charging. If you already have an alert radio and want a second radio with a large battery, the RunningSnail MD-090P is a good choice, and the FosPower A1 is a sensible low-cost spare for a car.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, charge it now, test the alert, and put a power bank next to it in the kit. That combination gives you warnings, news and phone power without asking a hand crank to do a job it was never built for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a radio without an alert function</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many budget crank radios receive NOAA broadcasts but do not listen for the warning tone. They tell you the forecast when you switch them on, but will not wake you for a tornado warning at night. If the radio is your main warning device, confirm the listing says alert, not just weather band.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting on the crank to charge a phone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hand cranks and small solar panels put very little energy into a phone, and makers such as Midland say their radios may not charge newer phones at all. Keep a charged power bank in the kit for phones and let the radio do radio, alert and light.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing the radio with a flat battery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lithium packs self-discharge in storage, and Midland notes that solar alone will not revive a dead ER310 battery. Charge the radio from a wall outlet or power bank every few months, and before storm season, so it is ready when you need it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never testing reception at home</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                NOAA reception varies by location. Turn the radio on, scan all seven weather channels, and note the strongest one before an emergency. Test the alert switch too. Finding out during a storm that your house is in a dead spot is the worst time to learn it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving backup batteries in the radio for years</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Alkaline cells can leak over long storage and corrode the contacts, which can ruin a radio you were counting on. Store backup AA or AAA cells in a bag next to the radio, check their dates each season, and replace them on a schedule.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

