          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best hand crank radio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most households, the Midland ER310. It has an automatic NOAA weather alert, a long rated runtime, a Cree LED flashlight and four power sources, including six AA batteries as a backup. For a smaller go-bag radio, the Eton American Red Cross FRX3+ adds a AAA backup in a lighter body.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do hand crank radios really work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for what they are designed to do. A crank reliably powers the radio and light for short periods when the battery is flat. It is not an efficient way to charge a phone or fill the internal battery. Charge the radio from an outlet or power bank beforehand and use the crank as a backup.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a hand crank radio charge a phone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only a little, and slowly. Midland says its ER310 will not reliably charge newer iPhones and Android phones, and that a 2,600mAh battery may barely raise a newer phone’s charge. For phones, keep a charged 10,000 to 20,000mAh power bank in your kit and treat the radio’s USB port as a last resort.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a NOAA weather radio and a weather alert radio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A NOAA weather radio receives the National Weather Service broadcasts on seven channels. A weather alert radio also listens for the warning tone while silent and sounds a siren when a warning is issued. Every alert radio is a NOAA radio, but not every NOAA radio has an alert.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a crank radio battery last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the radio and how loud you play it. Midland rates the ER310 for up to 45 hours of normal use and the ER210 for up to 25 hours. Midland also says a charged ER310 in the sun can last about two to three weeks between charges, versus about a week without sun.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are solar crank radios worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, as a backup to the internal battery. The small panel extends a charged battery rather than filling an empty one; Midland says solar alone will not charge a dead ER310. A radio that also takes AA or AAA batteries is the most reliable choice for long outages.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where should I keep an emergency radio?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Somewhere central and easy to reach in the dark, with spare batteries stored next to it, not inside it. Many people keep one in the household kit and a smaller one in the car. If you rely on its alert, keep it switched on in alert mode near where you sleep during storm season.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
